This directory contains the build variant and the bundled config.json for InterCom,
an unofficial, modified build of Element Desktop / Element Web.

InterCom is not affiliated with, endorsed by or supported by Element (New Vector Ltd).
"Element" is a trademark of New Vector Ltd and is deliberately not used in the name,
icon or branding of this build. The upstream licence and copyright notices are kept.

build.json electron-builder variant (app id, executable name, protocols)
config.json config baked into webapp.asar (brand, theme, no Element services)
icon.svg source of every icon; regenerate the rasters with gen-icons.sh

Building (Linux)
----------------

intercom/build.sh web app + webapp.asar + dist/linux-unpacked and a tar.gz
intercom/build.sh --skip-web reuse the existing apps/web/webapp
Set WEBPACK_JOBS=<n> to cap the minifier's worker processes when memory is tight.

INTERCOM_DIST_DIR=dist-next intercom/build.sh build beside dist/ while the app in dist/ is running
INTERCOM_TARGET=win intercom/build.sh Windows x64 installer into dist-win/, from Linux (see below)

Profile and environment
-----------------------

The profile lives in ~/.config/InterCom (never ~/.config/Element). INTERCOM_PROFILE_DIR and
INTERCOM_DESKTOP_CONFIG_JSON replace Element's ELEMENT_PROFILE_DIR / ELEMENT_DESKTOP_CONFIG_JSON,
and a ~/.config/InterCom/config.json overrides the bundled config.json (homeserver, theme, ...).

Hardware video on Linux (Electron 44 / Chromium 152)
----------------------------------------------------

Enabled by default (src/intercom.ts): AcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxGL, AcceleratedVideoEncoder,
VaapiOnNvidiaGPUs, VaapiIgnoreDriverChecks. Opt out with --no-hw-video or INTERCOM_NO_HW_VIDEO=1;
--hw-video-zero-copy additionally enables AcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxZeroCopyGL.

- Decode goes through VA-API. With NVIDIA this needs nvidia-vaapi-driver, and VaapiOnNvidiaGPUs
  is what makes Chromium accept it.
- Encode needs a VA driver with encode entrypoints (Intel, AMD). nvidia-vaapi-driver only
  decodes and Chromium has no NVENC backend on Linux, so on NVIDIA calls and screen shares are
  encoded in software whatever the flags.
- The Wayland screen capturer is PipeWire through xdg-desktop-portal by default; there is no
  flag for it any more.

Audio
-----

- WebRtcAllowInputVolumeAdjustment is disabled, so calls never move the system capture volume.
- Chromium does not list PulseAudio/PipeWire _monitor_ sources as microphones. To share what plays
  into a sink, the share dialog lists the sinks ("Apps playing into…", with the applications
  playing into each) and src/intercom-share-audio.ts makes a real source of the chosen ones:
  pactl load-module module-remap-source master=<sink>.monitor source_name=intercom-share-<sink>
  A remap of the same monitor that is already loaded (your own, e.g. from pipewire-pulse.conf.d)
  is used as it is and never unloaded; what InterCom loaded is unloaded when the source leaves the
  mix, when the share ends and when the app quits. Needs pactl; without it the section is hidden.
- Sources described as InterCom-Share-_, InterCom-Screen-_ or "InterCom share: *" are share sources:
  they are offered for a share's audio and kept out of the microphone list.
- Element Call reaches this through the widget action io.intercom.share_audio, which Element Web
  answers for the bundled call widget only (apps/web/src/intercom/ShareAudioBridge.ts).

Network
-------

HTTP/3 (QUIC) is off by default: src/intercom.ts appends --disable-quic. Behind some reverse proxies
and tunnels uploads over HTTP/3 crawl and time out (measured: about 10 KB/s behind Traefik in a GRE
tunnel, where HTTP/2 to the same server did 0.7 to 3.7 MB/s), and Chromium does not fall back.
--enable-quic or INTERCOM_ENABLE_QUIC=1 leaves it on. The app_start record of the diagnostics log
says which it was ("quic").

An upload that fails without an answer from the server (or with a 5xx, 408 or 429) is tried once
more after 1.5 s before "Upload Failed" is shown. Every failure is an upload_failed record in the
diagnostics log: HTTP status, kind of error, protocol (h2, h3, http/1.1), never a name or a URL.

Updates
-------

A release build updates itself from the releases of github.com/c4g7-dev/InterCom
(src/intercom-updater.ts, src/intercom-updater-core.ts). It asks the GitHub API for the releases
30 s after it started and every 6 hours, takes the newest tag of the form v<Element
version>-intercom.<n> (drafts, pre-releases and the android-v… releases are passed over), downloads
this platform's file and checks it against the release's SHA256SUMS. Then a toast offers "Restart
to update"; nothing is installed before that click. Settings > InterCom has the switch for the
automatic check, "Check now" and the state.

- The app knows its own version from "intercomVersion" in its package.json, which the build
  writes from INTERCOM_VERSION (tools/release.sh sets it). The package version stays Element's.
  A build without it, an unpackaged run and a run with --no-update never look for updates.
- Linux (the unpacked tar.gz): the archive is unpacked beside the install directory as
  <dir>.new. On restart <dir> becomes <dir>.old (an older backup is deleted) and <dir>.new
  becomes <dir>: two renames in one directory. Then the app starts again with the switches it
  was started with; links it was opened with are left out. To go back: quit, and rename
  <dir>.old to <dir>.
  The directory and its parent must be writable. Where they are not (a package under /opt, an
  AppImage) nothing is downloaded and the toast leads to the release page.
- A launcher script should export INTERCOM_LAUNCHER=<its own absolute path>: the app is then
  started again through it instead of through its executable, so that what the launcher sets
  up is set up again. It gets no arguments unless the launcher also exports the ones it was
  given, one per line, in INTERCOM_LAUNCHER_ARGS:

      INTERCOM_LAUNCHER="$(readlink -f "$0")"
      INTERCOM_LAUNCHER_ARGS="$(printf '%s\n' "$@")"
      export INTERCOM_LAUNCHER INTERCOM_LAUNCHER_ARGS
      exec /path/to/intercom/intercom --ozone-platform-hint=auto "$@"

    Without the variable the executable is started with the arguments it had, the launcher's
    switches among them, but not through the launcher.

- Windows (NSIS, per user): the installer is downloaded to %APPDATA%\InterCom\updates and
  checked. On restart it is run with "--updated /S --force-run", which installs silently and
  starts the app again.
- Why not electron-updater or Squirrel: the builds are not code signed, so neither could
  verify more than the checksum does; both take the package version for the app's version,
  which here is Element's; and electron-updater's GitHub provider reads the repository's
  "latest" release, which may be an Android one.
- SHA256SUMS comes from the same release as the file. It catches a broken download, not a
  release that somebody tampered with.
- Every step is an "updater" record in the diagnostics log (init, check, update_found,
  download_start, download_verified, staged, ready, install_linux, install_windows, error).
- Downloads are kept in ~/.cache/intercom-updates (XDG_CACHE_HOME) on Linux and removed once
  the release they are of is running.

Call shortcuts
--------------

In a call, with the window focused: Ctrl+Shift+M mutes, Ctrl+Shift+D deafens (nobody is heard and
the microphone is off, with a banner over the call) and Ctrl+Shift+V toggles the camera. While a
call is on, Ctrl+Shift+D is not the space panel's.

From anywhere: a second `intercom` with one of --toggle-mute, --mute, --unmute, --toggle-deafen,
--toggle-camera, --leave-call hands the action to the running instance and exits without opening a
window (and does nothing where none is running). Wayland compositors give applications no global
shortcuts, so bind the commands there, e.g. in hyprland.conf:

    bind = SUPER, M, exec, /path/to/run-intercom.sh --toggle-mute
    bind = SUPER SHIFT, M, exec, /path/to/run-intercom.sh --toggle-deafen

On Windows, Ctrl+Shift+M and Ctrl+Shift+D are global while a call is on; --no-global-shortcuts or
INTERCOM_NO_GLOBAL_SHORTCUTS=1 turns that off. --global-shortcuts-portal (Linux, experimental) asks
the XDG GlobalShortcuts portal for the same two.

Diagnostics
-----------

Every run writes <profile>/logs/diag-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.jsonl (~/.config/InterCom/logs on Linux,
%APPDATA%\InterCom\logs on Windows): one JSON record per line, 200 MB in total, oldest deleted
first. It holds

- an app_start record (versions, OS, Wayland/X11, feature switches, GPU feature status),
- every console line of every frame of every window that starts with "[intercom-diag] ", which
  is how Element Call reports the statistics of every track every 2 s and what happens in a call
  (src/intercom-diagnostics.ts here, src/diagnostics/ in element-call),
- console warnings and errors of all frames, with anything that looks like a token taken out.

Frame snapshots taken from the call's diagnostics overlay land in logs/snapshots/.
Help > "Open diagnostics folder" (also in the tray menu) opens the folder; Ctrl+Alt+D in a call
toggles the overlay; ../../../../tools/diag-summary.py turns one or more logs into verdicts.

Windows build from Linux
------------------------

INTERCOM_TARGET=win intercom/build.sh --skip-web builds dist-win/InterCom-Setup-x64.exe (NSIS,
per user, x64) and dist-win/win-unpacked. It needs wine on the PATH, for the executable's icon
and version details and for NSIS to write its uninstaller; unset DISPLAY and WAYLAND_DISPLAY to
keep wine off the desktop, and set WINEPREFIX to keep it out of ~/.wine.
On Windows itself (Git Bash) the same command needs no wine; the release workflow of the InterCom
repository builds the installer that way.

- It is not code signed, so SmartScreen warns on first start.
- The seshat search index is included when its Windows prebuilt is installed, which pnpm leaves
  out on Linux unless asked: pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --os linux --os win32 --cpu x64
  --filter element-desktop. Without it the app runs but cannot search encrypted rooms.
- The optional PKCS#11 modules cannot be compiled for Windows here and are left out.
